  "configuration_id": "config_003",
  "reason": "Evidence: config_002 (rna_only, multiprofile, pca_n2000, avgpool) achieved 0.591 acc vs config_001 (singleprofile) at 0.485, showing a strong +10.6% gain from multiprofile aggregation. However, we only know avgpool works; we don't know whether a learned aggregation (transformer) captures inter-profile interactions that simple averaging misses. Question: config_003 changes only the aggregation method (avgpool → transformer) while holding model setting, profile setting, and RNA encoder identical to config_002, giving a clean one-variable comparison. Why best next: (1) it directly exploits the strongest signal in the history (multiprofile benefit), (2) it isolates a single design axis so the result unambiguously attributes any change to aggregation, (3) the answer will inform the aggregation choice for all subsequent multiprofile and multimodal experiments, reducing uncertainty across a large portion of the remaining search space, and (4) it avoids confounding by not simultaneously changing encoder, modality, or fusion."
}

